# Protein Structure and Enzymes
#
# How a chain of amino acids finds its fold, and how a folded enzyme makes slow chemistry fast by holding the transition state.

#>cell_biology_proteins
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!cell_biology
#:form #!ledger
#:level #!advanced
#:review #!sourced
#:valence #!bone

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Protein Structure and Enzymes"
 ~#claim: "Sequence sets the fold under cellular conditions, and enzymes speed reactions mainly by binding the transition state more tightly than the substrate."
 ~#proof: "Anfinsen refolding experiments, the Michaelis and Menten rate law, and catalytic proficiencies measured by Radzicka and Wolfenden."
 ~#door: "Read nucleic acids and genomes next, then transport and signaling."
 ~#audience: "readers with introductory chemistry"
 continuity: "Protein Structure and Enzymes | transition state"
 cold_open: "An enzyme grips the transition state harder than the substrate, so a reaction that would take ages happens in a blink."
}

